# Not a checker bug but a limit of its design: a function's result is one type for all calls with arguments of the
# same types, so a result that depends on an argument's value (a filter here) is the union over all values.
# Here: "String.upcase: argument 1 must be String, but can be Hash" at line 15 (and Hash.size at 16); Ruby prints
# "A" and 1.
# Seen in the blog: ActionController::Parameters.expect(params, article: [:title, :body]) is a Parameters in Rails
# (a Hash filter keeps only nested parameters; otherwise ParameterMissing), but to the checker it is any value the
# parameters hold, and Parameters.to_hash(article_params) rejected the scalars (strong_parameters.sake:131, 132).
# Workaround in the blog's article_params / comment_params: `ps => ActionController::Parameters`.
def pick(h, key)
  v = h[key]
  raise KeyError, "param is missing: #{key}" if v == nil
  v
end
params = Hash.of("title" => "a", "article" => Hash.of("n" => 1))
p(String.upcase(pick(params, "title")))
p(Hash.size(pick(params, "article")))
